hypotheses.statement:
For every $l\ge1$, the polynomial $h_l$ belongs to $\mathbb Z[x]$, is intersective, and has degree $k$ and positive leading coefficient $a_l$. Every coefficient of $h_l$ is $O_h(a_l)$. For every prime $p$, the element $\mathfrak z_{p,l}=(\mathfrak z_p-r_l)/l$ belongs to $\mathbb Z_p$, and $$\label{local:centered-expansion}
 \begin{aligned}
 h_l(\mathfrak z_{p,l}+X)
   &=\sum_{j=m_p}^k b_{j,p}\frac{l^j}{\lambda(l)}X^j,\\
 v_p\!\left(b_{m_p,p}\frac{l^{m_p}}{\lambda(l)}\right)
   &=v_p(b_{m_p,p}).
 \end{aligned}$$ In particular, the root multiplicity remains $m_p$, and Condition <a href="#local:unit-condition" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="local:unit-condition">[local:unit-condition]</a>, when it holds for $h$, holds for every $h_l$ at these roots. For all positive integers $l,D$, there is an integer $i$, $0\le i<D$, such that $$\label{eq:1}
 \begin{gathered}
 h_l(Dx-i)=\lambda(D)h_{lD}(x),\\
 D\mid\lambda(D),\qquad \lambda(D)\le D^k,
 \qquad \mathop{\mathrm{rad}}(\lambda(D))=\mathop{\mathrm{rad}}(D).
 \end{gathered}$$ In particular, the argument $Dx-i$ is positive whenever $x\in\mathbb N_+$.

For all sufficiently large primes $p$, uniformly in $l\ge1$, define $$\begin{aligned}
 M_{l,p}&=\sum_{\substack{S\subseteq[w]\\|S|\le t+1}}
                 p^{-|S|\delta/2}\nu_{l,p,S},\\
 \mu_{l,p}&=\frac{M_{l,p}}{M_{l,p}(1)},\\
 \varepsilon_{l,p}&=\frac{1}{M_{l,p}(1)}
       \sum_{|S|=t+1}p^{-(t+1)\delta/2}\nu_{l,p,S}.
\end{aligned}$$ Then $\mu_{l,p}$ is a reflection-positive probability law and $0\le\varepsilon_{l,p}\le\mu_{l,p}$. Its exceptional mass satisfies $$\eta_{l,p}:=\varepsilon_{l,p}(1)\le p^{-4}.$$ The probability law $$\mu_{l,p}^{\circ}
       =\frac{\mu_{l,p}-\varepsilon_{l,p}}{1-\eta_{l,p}}$$ has only admissible increments $z_{v_{j+1}}-z_{v_j}$ along the designated cycle. Every component and both measures $\mu_{l,p}$, $\varepsilon_{l,p}$ are invariant under simultaneous translation of all labels. Every positive-mass component or subsum therefore has uniform single-slot marginals after normalization. In either $\mu_{l,p}$ or $\mu_{l,p}^{\circ}$, the total probability of components with $S\ne\varnothing$ is $O(p^{-\delta/2})$.

For every $l\ge1$, every sufficiently large prime $p$, and every integer $D\ge1$ with $p\nmid D$, the following holds. For each $a'\in\mathbb F_p$, the map $$(z_v)_{v\in V}\longmapsto
       \bigl(\lambda(D)^{-1}(z_v-a')\bigr)_{v\in V}
 \label{eq:7}$$ pushes $\mu_{l,p}$ to $\mu_{lD,p}$ and $\varepsilon_{l,p}$ to $\varepsilon_{lD,p}$.

The map $g\mapsto\|g\|_{l,\mathcal P}$ is a seminorm on the real functions on $X_{\mathcal P}$. It satisfies $$|\mathbb Eg|\le\|g\|_{l,\mathcal P},
 \qquad
 \|\mathbb E_Bg\|_{l,\mathcal P\setminus B}
 =\|\mathbb E_Bg\|_{l,\mathcal P}
 \le\|g\|_{l,\mathcal P}.$$ Translations of the input are isometries. Moreover, if $D$ is a positive integer coprime to every prime of $\mathcal P$, then for any $a'\in X_{\mathcal P}$, $$\left\|g\bigl(\lambda(D)^{-1}(\,\cdot-a')\bigr)
       \right\|_{l,\mathcal P}
 =\|g\|_{lD,\mathcal P}.
 \label{lift:transport}$$ Here the affine change is interpreted separately at each prime.

Let $\mathcal P$ be any finite set of primes, $Q\ge1$, and let $I$ be a consecutive integer interval with $|I|\ge10Q^6$. For every integer $r\ge1$, every $\epsilon>0$, every function $f:I\to\mathbb C$, and every subfamily $\mathcal F\subseteq\{B\subseteq\mathcal P:q_B\le Q\}$, $$\left\|\sum_{B\in\mathcal F}f_B\right\|_{2r}
 \le C_{r,\epsilon}\|f\|_\infty Q^\epsilon.
 \label{eq:12}$$ The constant is independent of the prime set, the interval and its location, the function, and the subfamily.

Let $I\subseteq[N]$ be a nonempty integer interval, let $B\subseteq\mathcal P_N$, and put $D=M_0q_B$. Fix $s\in\mathbb Z/M_0\mathbb Z$ and $b=(b_p)_{p\in B}\in X_B$. Let $g$ be the function on $X_{\mathcal P_N\setminus B}$ obtained by specializing $\mathcal L_{I,\mathcal P_N,Q}f_s$ at $z_B=b$. For every remaining-coordinate frequency $\xi$ with $q(\xi)q_B\le Q$, one has $$\label{eq:25}
 \widehat g(\xi)
 =\frac{D}{|I|}
   \sum_{\substack{x\in I\\x\equiv a'\pmod D}}
       \mathbf 1_A(x)e(-x\xi),$$ where $a'\pmod D$ is determined by $a'\equiv s\pmod{M_0}$ and $a'\equiv b_p\pmod p$ for $p\in B$.

Let $N$ be sufficiently large, let $l\ge1$, and let $A\subseteq[N]$ satisfy <a href="#eq:24" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:24">[eq:24]</a>. Suppose that $B\subseteq\mathcal P_N$ and $q_B\le N^\tau$. Set $$\begin{gathered}
 \mathcal S=\mathcal P_N\setminus B,
 \qquad D=M_0q_B,
 \qquad L_D=\lambda(D),\\
 n_B=\left\lceil\frac{N}{L_D}\right\rceil,
 \qquad \Lambda_B=\frac{L_Dn_B}{N}.
\end{gathered}$$ For each $v\in V$, choose $s_v\in\mathbb Z/M_0\mathbb Z$ and specialize $G_{s_v}$ at an arbitrary vector of coordinates on $B$, obtaining a function $g_v$ on $X_{\mathcal S}$. Then $$\label{eq:26}
 \bigl|\mathcal Z_{l,\mathcal S}((g_v)_{v\in V})\bigr|
 \le \Lambda_B^d\mathcal Y_{lD}(n_B)+O(N^{-\sigma}).$$ The scale and the rounding factor satisfy $1\le n_B<N$ and $$\label{eq:27}
 1\le\Lambda_B\le1+M_0^kN^{k\tau-1}.$$ The implied constant and the threshold for $N$ are independent of $l,A,B$ and all specializations.

Let $N$ be sufficiently large, let $l\le N^\zeta$, and let $A\subseteq[N]$ satisfy <a href="#eq:24" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:24">[eq:24]</a>. If $(s_v)_{v\in V}$ is good for $l$, then $$\label{eq:29}
 \left|
  \int\prod_{v\in V}G_{s_v}(z_v)\,
    d\bigotimes_{p\in\mathcal P_N}
       (\mu_{l,p}-\varepsilon_{l,p})
 \right|\ll N^{-\sigma}.$$ The bound is uniform in $l,A$ and the good assignment.

There are constants $C_1\ge1$ and $N_1\ge3$, depending only on the fixed polynomial $h$, its chosen roots, and the fixed structural choices, such that for every integer $N\ge N_1$ and every positive integer $l\le N^\zeta$, $$\begin{split}
 \mathcal Y_l(N)\le C_1N^{-\sigma}
 +(1+u_N)\Bigg(&
   (1-\rho_l)\mathcal Y_{lD_\varnothing}(n_\varnothing)\\
 &+\rho_l
   \sum_{\substack{\varnothing\ne B\subseteq\mathcal P_N\\q_B\le N^\tau}}
      \eta_{l,B}\mathcal Y_{lD_B}(n_B)\Bigg),
 \end{split}
 \label{eq:31}$$ where $$u_N=\bigl(1+M_0^kN^{k\tau-1}\bigr)^d-1\longrightarrow0.$$ Every scale $n_B$ appearing on the right belongs to $[1,N-1]$.

